# Who github asks for a review. This is not the ownership the rules mean.
#
# GR11's ownership is per file and per pilot, decided by whose commit created
# the file, and no list could keep up with it - a pilot writes their first trap
# and owns it the moment it lands. The referee reads that off git, which is
# why it is trustworthy. CODEOWNERS cannot, so it does not try.
#
# What it can do is make sure a human looks at the two places where a mistake
# is expensive and a red line has no override: the machinery the rules stand
# on, and the one corner of the project that belongs to one person.

# GR10 - the rules, and everything that enforces them. Changing these is
# allowed and lands in its own commit with a Rule-Change line. Somebody should
# still read it.
/GOLDEN_RULES.md          @durchnull
/CLAUDE.md                @durchnull
/tools/                   @durchnull
/.githooks/               @durchnull
/.github/                 @durchnull
/.gitattributes           @durchnull
/.claude/                 @durchnull

# GR13 - the owner's ground. The orb, and the song it is listening to. No
# budget and no override; the way through is to take the idea to the owner,
# which is a minute of their evening and they will probably enjoy it.
/docs/chronicle-song.js   @durchnull

# GR11 - the traps. The referee already refuses a commit that touches somebody
# else's, whoever sends it. This is the belt beside that brace.
/src/events/              @durchnull

# GR5 - the commons. Add to it freely; the review is for the things that are
# not additions.
/src/core/                @durchnull
/src/game/events.js       @durchnull
/src/game/profile.js      @durchnull
/src/game/tally.js        @durchnull
